Prerequisites

  1. Node.js (v16 or higher)
  2. PostgreSQL (v12 or higher) installed locally
  3. npm or yarn package manager

  Setup Instructions

  1. Database Setup (PostgreSQL)

  Start PostgreSQL service:
  # macOS (if installed via Homebrew)
  brew services start postgresql

  # Linux
  sudo service postgresql start

  # Windows
  # Start PostgreSQL from Services or pgAdmin

  Create database and user:
  # Connect to PostgreSQL
  psql postgres

  # Run these SQL commands:
  CREATE DATABASE webhook_db;
  CREATE USER webhook_user WITH PASSWORD 'webhook_password';
  GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE webhook_db TO webhook_user;
  \q

  Run database migrations:
  cd /Users/sobhan/Claud/FAMILY-ACTIVITY-DEMO

  # Run migrations in order
  psql -h localhost -U webhook_user -d webhook_db -f
  database/migrations/001_initial_schema.sql
  psql -h localhost -U webhook_user -d webhook_db -f
  database/migrations/002_add_schema_fields.sql
  psql -h localhost -U webhook_user -d webhook_db -f
  database/migrations/003_admin_users.sql

  2. Backend Setup

  Navigate to backend directory:
  cd /Users/sobhan/Claud/FAMILY-ACTIVITY-DEMO/backend

  Install dependencies:
  npm install

  Create .env file in backend directory:
  cat > .env << 'EOF'
  NODE_ENV=development
  API_PORT=3000

  # PostgreSQL Configuration
  DATABASE_HOST=localhost
  DATABASE_PORT=5432
  DATABASE_NAME=webhook_db
  DATABASE_USER=webhook_user
  DATABASE_PASSWORD=webhook_password

  # Kafka Configuration (optional - can skip if not using Kafka)
  KAFKA_BROKERS=localhost:9092
  KAFKA_TOPIC_EVENTS=delivery-messages
  KAFKA_CONSUMER_GROUP=webhook-delivery-consumer

  # AWS Configuration (optional - can skip for local development)
  # AWS_REGION=us-east-1
  # AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your-key
  # AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your-secret
  EOF

  Start the backend server:
  npm run dev:api

  The backend API will start on http://localhost:3000

  3. Frontend Setup

  Open a new terminal and navigate to frontend directory:
  cd /Users/sobhan/Claud/FAMILY-ACTIVITY-DEMO/frontend

  Install dependencies:
  npm install

  Create .env file in frontend directory:
  cat > .env << 'EOF'
  VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000/api/v1
  EOF

  Start the frontend development server:
  npm run dev

  The frontend will start on http://localhost:3001

  4. Create Initial Admin User

  Open a new terminal and run:
  cd /Users/sobhan/Claud/FAMILY-ACTIVITY-DEMO/backend

  # Create admin user via API
  curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/v1/admin/users \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{
      "name": "Admin User",
      "email": "admin@example.com",
      "password": "admin123",
      "role": "super_admin"
    }'

  Save the API key returned in the response!

  5. Access the Application

  1. Open browser: Navigate to http://localhost:3001
  2. Login: Use the email and password you just created
  3. API Key: The API key from step 4 will be used automatically after login

  Stopping the Application

  Stop backend:
  # Press Ctrl+C in the backend terminal

  Stop frontend:
  # Press Ctrl+C in the frontend terminal

  Stop PostgreSQL (optional):
  # macOS
  brew services stop postgresql

  # Linux
  sudo service postgresql stop

  Troubleshooting

  Port already in use:
  # Kill process on port 3000 (backend)
  lsof -i :3000 | grep LISTEN | awk '{print $2}' | xargs kill

  # Kill process on port 3001 (frontend)
  lsof -i :3001 | grep LISTEN | awk '{print $2}' | xargs kill

  Database connection issues:
  - Verify PostgreSQL is running: psql -h localhost -U webhook_user -d webhook_db
  - Check credentials in .env match your database setup
  - Ensure database webhook_db exists

  Missing dependencies:
  # Backend
  cd backend && npm install

  # Frontend
  cd frontend && npm install

  That's it! Your application should now be running locally without Docker.